---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import AboutShell from '../../components/AboutShell.astro';
import { useTranslations } from '../../i18n/ui';

const t = useTranslations('en');
const description =
  'Senior full-stack engineer. Capability architecture, production infrastructure, and AI developer tooling.';
---

<BaseLayout title={`${t('about.title')} — Cristopher Martinez`} description={description}>
  <SiteHeader />
  <AboutShell heading={t('about.title')} lede={description}>
    <h2>Systems, not features</h2>
    <p>
      I split a video platform into eight capability packages that talk to each other through
      published contracts. Products import only the public index of each capability — internal
      subpaths are blocked by a lint rule rather than by convention, because a boundary nobody
      enforces stops being a boundary. Dependencies are wired in a single composition root and
      passed down as ports.
    </p>

    <h2>I ship it and I run it</h2>
    <p>
      Two products answer on their own domains today, on infrastructure I operate: VPS, Docker,
      nginx, Prometheus and Grafana, across five deployment pipelines. The monorepo carries 1,923
      commits and 258 test files.
    </p>
    <p>
      I also audited it against myself — thirteen modules, fifty-eight findings, eleven of them
      critical, ordered by a dependency graph. That audit says more about how I work than clean
      code would: I go looking for my own mistakes before somebody else finds them.
    </p>

    <h2>AI as infrastructure, not as an API call</h2>
    <p>
      Most work described as "AI" means calling a chat endpoint. Mine sits a layer below that:
      renting multi-GPU boxes and picking offers by VRAM so a 32B model can be split across two
      GPUs with tensor parallelism; face embeddings clustered per tenant, where crossing a tenant
      boundary throws rather than silently merging people; and an MCP server that gives coding
      agents durable project memory, with 181 tests behind it.
    </p>

    <h2>How I work</h2>
    <p>
      Concepts before code. I would rather spend an afternoon understanding why something breaks
      than an hour patching around it, because the patch comes back and the understanding does not.
      I write the check that fails when the logic breaks, and I keep the shortcuts visible instead
      of pretending they are not there.
    </p>
  </AboutShell>
</BaseLayout>
